#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097872 :tutorial/content "The **chain rule** for differentiation states that the derivative of a composite function $f(g(x))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nThere is some intuition behind this rule: if we think of the derivatives as fractions, the ${\\textrm{d}g}$'s would appear to cancel out.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see how the chain rule can be used to differentiate a function like \n\n$$\nf(x) = (1+2x)^3,\n$$\n\nfirst, notice that the function can be written as a composite function\n\n$$\nf(g) = g^3, \\qquad g(x) = 1+2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}g}({g}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+2x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6{g}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(x) = 1+2x$ for $g,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6(1+2x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll provide a derivation of the chain rule at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "743ad997-8732-5c0f-9676-ff1d44f3b513" :tutorial/math-academy-id 415 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097873 :tutorial/content "The chain rule can also be expressed using primes instead of Leibniz notation.\n\nThe chain rule, in prime notation, states that\n\n$$\n(f(g(x)))' = f'(g(x)) \\cdot g'(x).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58c36c82-b739-55f9-8e99-4df1e0cbde24" :tutorial/math-academy-id 418 :tutorial/title "Another Notation for the Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097933 :tutorial/content "Often, differential equations aren't easy to solve. Some are so difficult that they can only be solved using entire rooms full of very powerful computers! \n\nHowever, there is one type that's often quite easy. These are differential equations that can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = g(x).\n$$\n\nAs an example, suppose that we want to find the solution to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{1+2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this differential equation by integrating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nFor the left-hand side, calculating the indefinite integral of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ gives $y+C_1$ because differentiation is reversed by integration. For the right-hand side, this is a reasonably standard integral that we know how to compute. So, we get\n\n$$\ny + C_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln|1+2x| + C_2.\n$$\n\nWe now tidy up the result, solving for $y$ and combining the two constants $C_1$ and $C_2$ into a third constant $C=C_2-C_1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+{C}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+2x|+{C}_{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+2x|+({C}_{2}-{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution we obtained is called a **general solution** because it involves the arbitrary constant $C.$ This is in contrast to \"particular\" solutions, in which the constant is set to a fixed value. We will learn more about particular solutions later, but for now we will focus on general solutions.\n\n**Note:** Remember that we combined the constants $C_1$ and $C_2$ into a single constant $C.$ For these types of differential equations, we can usually do this, and for that reason, we often go straight to adding a constant $C$ to one side of the equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "a136fcee-de1f-5f62-b419-01f7fc6b3279" :tutorial/math-academy-id 952 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097934 :tutorial/content "Often, instead of the general solution that involves an arbitrary constant, we want a **particular solution**. A particular solution is a solution that satisfies the differential equation *and* some other condition on the function.  \n\nAs a simple example, consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = 2x.\n$$\n\nWe can find the general solution by integrating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn its current form, the function $y$ represents infinitely many solutions, each with a different value of the arbitrary constant $C.$ \n\nHowever, suppose we want to find a **particular solution** that satisfies the condition $y(3)=10.$ To do this, we can solve for the appropriate value of the arbitrary constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}+C \\\\ 10 & ={3}^{2}+C \\\\ 10 & =9+C \\\\ 1 & =C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired particular solution is\n\n$$\ny = x^2 + 1.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c3298ec-d6a5-5792-9ebc-90f1b44de891" :tutorial/math-academy-id 957 :tutorial/title "Particular Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100728} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100894 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100732} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102032 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find a solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = (1-2x)y^2 \\,,\n$$\n\nsatisfying the additional condition $y(0)=1.$ This additional condition $y(0)=1$ is called an **initial condition** or sometimes a **boundary condition** of the differential equation. \n\nFirst of all, we note that the given differential equation is separable, so we can separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(1-2x){y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1-2x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{{y}^{2}}\\, & =\\int (1-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =x-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & ={x}^{2}-x-C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-C}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny    =   \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x - C} \\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNow we choose the constant $C$ such that the condition $y(0)=1$ is satisfied. Substituting $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the general solution and solving for $C,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) &=\\frac{1}{0-0-C}=1\\,⇒\\,C=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of $y' = (1-2x)y^2$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny    =   \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x - (-1)} = \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x + 1}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo recap: the solution of a first-order separable ordinary differential equation (ODE) with an initial condition is obtained by \n\n1\\. separating the variables,\n\n2\\. integrating the equation, and then\n\n3\\. substituting the condition into the solution to solve for the constant of integration." :tutorial/id #uuid "e2c4c33d-a1bd-537e-9daa-1960f765953d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 974 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124673 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124373} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ade69467-c229-509d-9bdc-b28935b2d1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Growth Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124674 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124370} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "53776591-13cc-549e-9f32-c5abaeced1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124371} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125711 :tutorial/content "Our general solution for the wolf population in month $t$ is $P(t) = 450 - Ke^{-kt}.$ Now, let's find the specific values of our constants.\n\nWe're told that, in month $6,$ the population was $392.$ So, applying the condition $P(6) = 392,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6) & =392 \\\\ 450-K{e}^{-6k} & =392 \\\\ K{e}^{-6k} & =58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, in month $12,$ the population was $420.$ So, applying the condition $P(12) = 420,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =420 \\\\ 450-K{e}^{-12k} & =420 \\\\ K{e}^{-12k} & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-6k}}{K{e}^{-12k}} & =\\frac{58}{30} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{29}{15} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{29}{15}\\right) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{29}{15}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =58{e}^{6k} \\\\  & =58\\left(\\frac{29}{15}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1682}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the wolf population in month $t$ is\n\n$$\nP(t) = 450 - \\frac{1682}{15}e^{-(t/6)\\ln(29/15)}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4cf455fa-240c-5cab-994e-c0447e7d8788" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14905 :tutorial/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth (Continued)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125810 :tutorial/content "Now that we’ve practiced identifying inhibited growth models, let’s work through an example to see how they are used in practice.\n\nSuppose a wildlife reserve is tracking the population of a reintroduced wolf pack. The environment can support a maximum population of $450$ wolves, but the current population is still below this limit. Since monitoring began, the population reached $392$ wolves after $6$ months and $420$ wolves after $12$ months.\n\nLet's construct an inhibited growth model to describe the wolf population in terms of the month $t.$\n\nThe rate at which the population $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum population the reserve can sustain, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=450,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k(450-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{450-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{450-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|450-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $450 - P > 0$ for all $t \\geq 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}450-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 450-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 450-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =450-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nThis is the general solution. On the next slide, we will use the population data to find the specific values for the constants $K$ and $k.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "91be4d2b-a528-55eb-a5f4-1e02178fad56" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4076 :tutorial/title "Modeling Inhibited Growth"}]}
